web   : http://www.ecs.umass.edu/~polizzi/feast  
/////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////


General Description: Solving Eigenvalue Problem of form AX=eBX or AX=eX for
                     obtaining eigenpairs within the search interval [Emin,Emax]


Reference: "Density-Matrix-Based Algorithm for Solving Eigenvalue Problems"
           E. Polizzi
           Physical Review B, Vol. 79, 11512 (2009)     
                                                                       

===================================================================================================
Version: 1.0   
Version Description: 

    * Solving Ax=ex or Ax=eBx, A is real symmetric or complex Hermitian, B is symmetric or Hermitian positive definite
    * SMP version (one node),
    * Source code and pre-compiled libraries provided for commun architectures. Fortran 90 and C compilers are both needed 
      to compile the source code. The pre-compiled libraries do not require any specific compilers (no runtime dependencies).
    * All FEAST interfaces compatible with Fortran (77 and 90) and C,
    * Reverse communication interfaces: Maximum flexibility for application-specific. Those are matrix format independent,
      inner system solver independent, so users must provide their own linear system solvers (direct or iterative) and 
      mat-vec utility routines.
    * Predefined driver interfaces for dense, banded, and sparse formats: Easy ("plug and play") and Optimized.
    * All FEAST libraries require BLAS and LAPACK packages (not included),
    * FEAST banded drivers use the SPIKE banded primitives (included)
    * FEAST sparse drivers use the PARDISO solver (not included).
    * FEAST dense drivers are not intended for performances (that would require the PFEAST version -- in preparation)
    * Real/Complex and Single/Double precisions (the use of double precision arithmetic is highly recommended)
    * Examples and documentation included,
    * Utility routines for sparse systems included: users can provide their sparse systems for quick testing, timing, etc. .
 ===================================================================================================



Library: FEASTROOT (this directory)
            |
  -----------------------------------------------------  
    |       |       |        |        |        |
   src     lib   include    doc    example     utility 


**********************************************************************************************
**********************************************************************************************
**************** In the following, we address the  question: *********************************
****************         How should you use FEAST ?                   ************************
**********************************************************************************************
**********************************************************************************************

===================================================================================================
ATTENTION : It is recommended to define the Shell variable "export FEASTROOT="path to this directory"
===================================================================================================


LIBRARY PATH: (choose one)
              -L/$(FEASTROOT)/lib/x64 (for 64 bits architectures)
              -L/$(FEASTROOT)/lib/ia64 (for Intel Itanium- 64 bits)
              -L/$(FEASTROOT)/lib/"yourown" (where "yourown" directory can contain the FEAST library
                                             compiled for your own specific architectures)

INCLUDE PATH: (It is mandatory only for F90 codes where one chooses to use the instruction --include "feast.fi"--
               in the header)
              -I/$(FEASTROOT)/include


COMPILATION LINKS: (choose one)
		   -lfeast_rci    (FEAST Reverse Communication Interfaces)
                   -lfeast_dense  (FEAST dense interfaces/drivers,  also includes -lfeast_rci)
                   -lfeast_banded (FEAST banded interfaces/drivers, also includes -lfeast_rci)
                   -lfeast_sparse (FEAST sparse interfaces/drivers, also includes -lfeast_rci)
                   -lfeast        (FEAST library that includes all of the above)
                   

DEPENDENCY:
                   - the libraries LAPACK/BLAS (not provided) are needed
                   - the library PARDISO (not provided) is only needed for -lfeast or -lfeast_sparse
                   - the Intel MKL library is highly recommended and contains pardiso,lapack and blas 
              

EXAMPLES of COMPILATION LINKS with DEPENDENCY: (choose one)

             include everything               1/ -lfeast -lpardiso -lapack -lblas
             include dense drivers+rci        2/ -lfeast_dense  -lapack -lblas
             include banded drivers+rci       3/ -lfeast_banded -lapack -lblas
             include sparse drivers+rci       4/ -lfeast_sparse -lpardiso -lapack -lblas
             include rci                      5/ -lfeast_rci -lapack -lblas


REVERSE COMMUNICATION INTERFACES (RCI): TFEAST_RCI ("T" denotes S,D,C or Z)
                    -routines are then called:
                          SFEAST_RCI, DFEAST_RCI (Real Symmetric resp. single and double precision) 
                          CFEAST_RCI, ZFEAST_RCI (Complex Hermitian resp. single and double precison)
                    -Matrix free format (FEAST is independent of the matrix data format) 
	            -users must then provide their own direct/iterative solver for solving the linear 
                     system-type  MY =F
                    -A preconditioner can be also used for iterative solver
                    -user must provide his own mat-vec
                    -for Complex systems, FEAST can be accelerated if user can provide a routine for
                     solving the linear system-type  M^H Y=F (H for Hermitian) knowing only the 
                     factorization of M (feastparam(11)=1 and option 21 for "ijob")

               
PREDEFINED DRIVER INTERFACES:  TFEAST_XYY 
                                          *"T"  denotes S or D for real single/double precision 
                                                        C or Z for complex single/double precision 
                                          *"YY" denotes GE for generalized eigenvalue problem
                                                        ST for standart eigenvalue problem  
                          
                    - If "X"=D :: Dense format  (ATTENTION: current version is not intended for obtaining performance)
                      uses LAPACK inner system solver:
                    - If "X"=B :: Banded LAPACK Format  
                      uses SPIKE primitives inner system solver (included)
                    - If "X"=S :: Sparse Format  
                      uses PARDISO inner system solver (not included)

                     REMARK: Using F90 with the header (include "feast.fi"), all drivers are simply named: FEAST 


FEAST and PARALLELISM:  
                    - Parallelism is not explicitly implemented in FEAST within one node 
                     i.e. linear systems are solved one after another (avoid the fight for resources/save memory)
                    - Parallelism is then implicitly obtained using the shared memory blas, lapack or pardiso
                    - If the pardiso solver is used the field "feastparam(10)" should be identical than 
                      the shell variable "OMP_NUM_THREADS".
                    - If the MKL library is used, the above field is obsolete and the shell variable
                      MKL_NUM_THREADS can be used for the dense/banded/sparse threaded libraries.
                    - NOTE: The PFEAST solver with explicit MPI parallelism for Clusters is in preparation. 
                      


